---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.1 — while Loops &amp; The WhileLab Algorithms Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.1, 2.7 · 90-Minute Design-First Build.</strong> A <code>while</code> loop
    repeats a block as long as a condition holds <code>true</code> — making it the right tool
    whenever you do <strong>not</strong> know in advance how many iterations it will take to finish.
    On the AP CS A exam, <code>while</code> loops show up in two signature patterns: state-driven
    simulations and decimal digit extraction (<code>% 10</code> and <code>/ 10</code>).
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Design-First Workflow (One-Shot Challenge):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/WhileLab.java" download>WhileLab.java</a> — starter file with Javadocs,
            stubs, and a built-in <strong>Swamp Survival &amp; Rune Decoder Bench</strong> in
            <code>main()</code>.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong>
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Design before coding:</strong> Read what each function simulates, inspect its
            parameters, and answer the Guided Design questions on infinite-loop guards, intra-round
            order, digit peeling order, and edge cases like <code>0</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Draft &amp; test in the sandbox:</strong> Draft your method code below, click
            <strong>Copy</strong> to paste into <code>WhileLab.java</code>, and check your outputs
            with:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>javac WhileLab.java
java WhileLab</code></pre>
    <p style="margin-top:0.4rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        <strong>Step 3 (One-Shot Pass):</strong> Once your sandbox handles every normal and edge
        case cleanly, unlock <code>WhileLabCheck.java</code> below and aim to pass all
        <strong>19 checks</strong> on your first run.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · State-Driven Survival &amp; Infinite Loop Guards</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>roundsToSurvivePoison(int startingHP, int poisonPerRound, int regenPerRound)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Your hero enters a toxic swamp wearing a regeneration amulet. The combat simulator needs
            to know how many rounds the hero endures before falling — and must detect beforehand if
            the hero will survive forever rather than hanging the program in an infinite loop!
        </p>
        <h4>Round Rules, Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li>If <code>startingHP &lt;= 0</code> on entry, return <code>0</code>.</li>
            <li>Each round: (1) subtract <code>poisonPerRound</code> from HP; (2) <em>only if HP is
                still strictly &gt; 0</em>, add <code>regenPerRound</code>. (If poison drops HP to
                <code>&lt;= 0</code>, the hero dies immediately before regen fires.)</li>
            <li>If the hero will survive forever, return <code>-1</code> without hanging.</li>
            <li>Examples: <code>(0, 5, 2) &rarr; 0</code>; <code>(15, 4, 0) &rarr; 4</code>;
                <code>(10, 4, 2) &rarr; 4</code>; <code>(5, 5, 10) &rarr; 1</code> (dies Round 1
                before regen!); <code>(10, 4, 4) &rarr; -1</code>; <code>(10, 0, 0) &rarr; -1</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>roundsToSurvivePoison</code></h3>
        <p>Plan your entry guards and round simulation before coding:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Round-1 Lethal vs. Infinite Regen Guard:</strong> Why does putting
                <code>if (regenPerRound &gt;= poisonPerRound) return -1;</code> at the very top of
                the method fail on <code>(5, 5, 10)</code> and <code>(0, 5, 2)</code>? State the
                exact guard order for <code>startingHP &lt;= 0</code>,
                <code>poisonPerRound &lt;= 0</code>, Round-1 survival
                (<code>startingHP - poisonPerRound &lt;= 0</code>), and infinite survival!</li>
            <li><strong>Loop Choice &amp; Step Order:</strong> Why does this require a
                <code>while</code> loop? What local variables do you initialize before the loop,
                what is the loop condition, and in what order must poison subtraction,
                <code>rounds++</code>, and conditional regen run inside one round?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>WhileLab.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>roundsToSurvivePoison</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>WhileLab.java</code>, and check all five poison cases in
                <code>java WhileLab</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 2 &amp; 3 · AP Digit Extraction (% 10 and / 10)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>sumRuneDigits(int runeCode)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Arcane lock runes are verified by summing their base-10 digits. Instead of converting
            an integer to a <code>String</code>, AP CS A expects you to peel decimal digits
            mathematically right-to-left using <code>% 10</code> (last digit) and <code>/ 10</code>
            (chop last digit).
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>int runeCode</code> (precondition: <code>runeCode &gt;= 0</code>).</li>
            <li>Examples: <code>4729 &rarr; 22</code> (<code>4 + 7 + 2 + 9</code>),
                <code>1005 &rarr; 6</code>, <code>8 &rarr; 8</code>, <code>0 &rarr; 0</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>sumRuneDigits</code></h3>
        <p>Plan the digit-peeling loop before coding:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Loop Condition &amp; Step Order:</strong> What local accumulator variable do
                you declare, what <code>while</code> condition continues as long as digits remain,
                and why would dividing <code>runeCode /= 10;</code> <em>before</em> extracting
                <code>runeCode % 10</code> break <code>4729</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>WhileLab.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>sumRuneDigits</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>WhileLab.java</code>, and check <code>4729</code>, <code>1005</code>, and
                <code>0</code> in <code>java WhileLab</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>countEvenDigits(int runeCode)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            A sister validator counts how many digits in <code>runeCode</code> are even
            (<code>0, 2, 4, 6, 8</code>). Remember that the integer <code>0</code> itself has one
            digit—and <code>0</code> is even!
        </p>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>4826 &rarr; 4</code>, <code>13579 &rarr; 0</code>,
                <code>20405 &rarr; 4</code>, <code>0 &rarr; 1</code>!</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>countEvenDigits</code></h3>
        <p>Compare this to <code>sumRuneDigits</code> and spot the two traps:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Similarities &amp; Premature Return Trap:</strong> How does the loop skeleton
                match <code>sumRuneDigits</code>? If you check each digit with
                <code>if (digit % 2 == 0) count++;</code>, what bug happens if you put an
                <code>else { return count; }</code> inside the loop on <code>20405</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>The Zero Edge Case:</strong> Why did <code>while (runeCode &gt; 0)</code>
                naturally work for <code>sumRuneDigits(0)</code>, yet return the wrong answer for
                <code>countEvenDigits(0)</code>—and what guard fixes it?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>WhileLab.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>countEvenDigits</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>WhileLab.java</code>, and check <code>20405</code> and <code>0</code> in
                <code>java WhileLab</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · AP Synthesis: Collatz Stabilization</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>manaCrystalSteps(int startingEnergy)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            An unstable mana crystal oscillates according to the Collatz (Hailstone) sequence until
            its energy settles at <code>1</code>: even energy divides by <code>2</code>; odd energy
            becomes <code>energy * 3 + 1</code>. Counting steps lets the wizard time when a crystal
            becomes safe to socket.
        </p>
        <h4>Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>1 &rarr; 0</code>, <code>0 &rarr; 0</code>,
                <code>16 &rarr; 4</code> (<code>16 &rarr; 8 &rarr; 4 &rarr; 2 &rarr; 1</code>),
                <code>6 &rarr; 8</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>manaCrystalSteps</code></h3>
        <p>Avoid the double-update bug inside one iteration:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong><code>if / else</code> vs. Two Separate <code>if</code>s:</strong> If you
                write two separate <code>if</code> statements inside the <code>while (energy &gt; 1)</code>
                loop (one checking odd, then one checking even) instead of <code>if / else</code>,
                trace what happens in one step when <code>energy = 3</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>WhileLab.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>manaCrystalSteps</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>WhileLab.java</code>, and test all four methods with
                <code>java WhileLab</code> before unlocking <code>WhileLabCheck.java</code> below?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All four methods produce expected results in <code>java WhileLab</code></li>
            <li>You unlock <code>WhileLabCheck.java</code> below and aim for <strong><code>19 passed, 0 failed</code></strong> on your first run</li>
            <li>You have downloaded your <code>.json</code> answers file for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/while-loops.html">while Loops</a> — initialization, test, update, infinite loop guards</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/arithmetic.html">Arithmetic &amp; Modulo</a> — % 10 and / 10 digit extraction</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="4.1" title="4.1 while Loops &amp; The WhileLab Algorithms Build — AP CS A" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
